1.3.5 ‘(node+olp TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ...)’
--------------------------------------------

Like ‘node+headline’ but navigates (or creates) an arbitrary outline
path.  Each heading in the path is found or created in sequence.

   If TITLE-OR-ID is ‘nil’, the user is prompted interactively.  Heading
strings may contain ‘${var}’ template variables.

     '("x" "Example" entry
       "** %?\n"
       :target (node+olp "my-node-id" "Section" "Subsection")
       :unnarrowed t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Next: (nodefunc+olp+datetree FUNCTION "h1" "h2" ),  Prev: (node+olp TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Up: New capture target types

1.3.6 ‘(node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ...)’
-----------------------------------------------------

Positions capture at an org datetree entry beneath the node.  The OLP
headings are optional: pass none to build the datetree directly under
the node, or pass one or more to navigate (or create) intermediate
headings first.

   The datetree is built by ‘org-datetree-find-*-create’ per the
‘:tree-type’ template property (default ‘day’; other values: ‘week’,
‘month’, a list grouping like ‘(year month day)’, or a function that
returns such a list).  Setting ‘:time-prompt t’ asks for the date
interactively; otherwise the current date is used (respecting
‘org-overriding-default-time’).

     ;; Daily log entry under Journal / YYYY / YYYY-MM / YYYY-MM-DD
     '("j" "Journal" plain
       "- %U %?"
       :target (node+olp+datetree "my-journal-id" "Log")
       :unnarrowed t)

     ;; Weekly retrospective, ask for date at capture time
     '("r" "Weekly retro" entry
       "** %?\n"
       :target (node+olp+datetree "my-journal-id")
       :tree-type week
       :time-prompt t
       :unnarrowed t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (nodefunc+olp+datetree FUNCTION "h1" "h2" ),  Prev: (node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Up: New capture target types

1.3.7 ‘(nodefunc+olp+datetree FUNCTION "h1" "h2" ...)’
------------------------------------------------------

Like ‘node+olp+datetree’, but the destination node is computed by
FUNCTION at capture time instead of looked up by ID or title.  All other
semantics (optional OLP, ‘:tree-type’, ‘:time-prompt’) are identical.

     '("d" "Daily note" plain
       "- %?"
       :target (nodefunc+olp+datetree my-daily-node-function "Log")
       :unnarrowed t)


